WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.530
Find the work worth automating, then pick the platform.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.190
This is the video for module one of Automation with Zapier and Make.

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:17.630
Zapier and Make move information from one app to another and act on it, the same way every time, without anyone pressing buttons.

4
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:26.730
They are not magic. If the work is messy, automation copies the mess faster. So your first skill is seeing the work clearly.

5
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:32.120
In this module you will learn to measure how many hours a task costs.

6
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.760
And how each platform counts usage, so you can predict the monthly cost.

7
00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:46.140
Learn these four tests by heart. First, the task repeats. It happens many times a week, not twice a year.

8
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:56.370
Second, it follows clear rules. You can write it as, when X happens, do Y. If the rule is, it depends on how I feel, it is not ready.

9
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:08.290
Third, the data is already digital, in a form, a spreadsheet or an email. Paper records must be typed first, and Zapier or Make cannot do that typing for you.

10
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.590
Fourth, mistakes cost something. A missed follow-up loses a sale.

11
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:21.960
Judgement calls, like refunds or angry complaints, stay with a person. Automation only gathers the facts and alerts her.

12
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:31.680
Ask the person who does the work to log one normal week: task, how often, minutes each time, apps involved, and what goes wrong.

13
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:39.050
Do not rely on memory. People forget the small, boring tasks, and those are often the best ones to automate.

14
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:47.490
Owners respond to hours and naira, not to the word automation. So multiply times per week by minutes each.

15
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.870
Multiply by 4 for a rough month.

16
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.190
Divide by 60 to get hours.

17
00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.820
If you know what an hour of staff time costs the business, multiply again, using the business's own figure.

18
00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:13.630
A common mistake is to inflate the savings. Someone still checks the sheet and fixes the odd failed run. So round down, call it an estimate, and show your working.

19
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:21.660
Here is a worked example. Funmi runs a catering business in Ibadan, and her assistant logged one week.

20
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:31.230
Copying 25 orders at 3 minutes each took 75 minutes. Sending 25 confirmation emails at 2 minutes each took 50 minutes.

21
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:44.150
Those two tasks are frequent and rule-based. Together they take 125 minutes a week, about 500 minutes, or roughly 8.3 hours, in a four-week month.

22
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:53.400
Funmi values her assistant's time at about 1,500 naira an hour. That is her own approximate figure.

23
00:02:54.650 --> 00:03:03.140
So automating those two tasks frees about 12,500 naira of staff time a month. Now she has a clear first target.

24
00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:14.330
Zapier and Make think the same way. A trigger is the event that starts the automation, such as a new form response. Each automation has one trigger.

25
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.380
A filter checks a rule and stops runs that should not continue.

26
00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:31.380
Then an action does the work, like sending an email. In it you map fields. You do not type a name into the email. You insert the Name field, so each run uses its own name.

27
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.400
Every run is saved in history to check later.

28
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:45.230
Typing a fixed value where a field should go is the most common beginner mistake. Every customer then gets the same name or amount.

29
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:59.000
Zapier counts tasks. A task is any successful action. Triggers, filters and Formatter steps do not use tasks. The free plan gives 100 tasks a month, at the time of writing.

30
00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:10.130
Make counts credits. Each module run is one credit by default, including every time a scheduled trigger checks for new data, even on quiet days.

31
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.600
The free plan gives 1,000 credits. So the schedule you choose changes the bill.

32
00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:30.820
To remember. Good tasks repeat, follow clear rules, start digital, and cost something when done wrong. A one-week log beats memory. Weekly minutes times 4, divided by 60, gives rough monthly hours.

33
00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:36.610
Map fields, never fixed values. And check the official pricing page on the day you quote.

34
00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:50.090
Now it is your turn. Run a repeat-task audit for a business you know, with permission. Map your top task on paper, field by field. Then cost it on both platforms and recommend one.

35
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.380
Continue with the lessons below.
